															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seanmichaelragan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1106688); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1106688&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609167965" alt="seanmichaelragan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1106688)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seanmichaelragan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1106688); return false;" >seanmichaelragan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1312997172 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Aug 2011 17:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1225788">
									

<p>Not sure that makes much sense. Acrylic is a plastic, and though its molecules may be in a &quot;glassy&quot; state as opposed to &quot;crystalline,&quot; I don't think such subtlety is expected in this context, and I think most people will be confused by &quot;plastic glass.&quot; &quot;Tempered glass,&quot; &quot;borosilicate glass,&quot; &quot;reinforced glass,&quot; &quot;armored glass,&quot; and &quot;glass laminate&quot; are suggested alternatives. Of these, I think I like &quot;armored glass&quot; best.</p>

							</div>

								
					
